# CONDUCTOR line-ending policy.
#
# Windows checkouts default to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every tracked
# text file to CRLF. That is not cosmetic here:
#
#   - The six adapters are POSIX shell scripts run through Git Bash, and hook
#     templates are emitted into adopter projects as executable .sh files.
#   - Role compilation rewrites managed files with line-anchored patterns such
#     as /^model:\s*\S+\s*$/m. Against CRLF the trailing \s* consumes the \r
#     while the replacement does not restore it, so one line silently loses its
#     carriage return, the file gains mixed endings, and its checksum no longer
#     matches what the transaction recorded.
#
# Normalizing to LF in the working tree on every platform removes that class at
# the source rather than teaching each rewrite to preserve whichever ending it
# happens to find.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the surfaces that must stay byte-identical across platforms:
# adapters, emitted hook templates, and every shipped instruction/config file.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.template  text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.csv       text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf

*.png binary
